"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,625\nThe Learning Brain. Series 4: Habits\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,314\nEpisode 9\n\n3\n00:00:07,394 --> 00:00:09,659\nDunning-Kruger, carefully\n\n4\n00:00:12,459 --> 00:00:17,188\nYou have probably heard of the Dunning Kruger effect: the idea that the less you know,\n\n5\n00:00:17,208 --> 00:00:18,864\nthe more confident you are.\n\n6\n00:00:19,227 --> 00:00:20,637\nIt is a great story.\n\n7\n00:00:20,928 --> 00:00:22,925\nThe evidence is more interesting.\n\n8\n00:00:23,095 --> 00:00:27,422\nIn 1999, Cornell students took tests of logic,\n\n9\n00:00:27,587 --> 00:00:29,007\ngrammar, and humor.\n\n10\n00:00:29,597 --> 00:00:33,198\nThose in the bottom quarter scored around the twelfth percentile,\n\n11\n00:00:33,504 --> 00:00:36,292\nbut estimated they were around the sixty second.\n\n12\n00:00:36,462 --> 00:00:39,193\nBut random numbers can draw the same picture.\n\n13\n00:00:39,694 --> 00:00:43,988\nWhen researchers fed computer generated scores into the classic graph,\n\n14\n00:00:44,226 --> 00:00:46,335\nthe famous pattern appeared anyway.\n\n15\n00:00:46,832 --> 00:00:48,197\nRegression to the mean,\n\n16\n00:00:48,357 --> 00:00:51,304\nplus most people rating themselves above average,\n\n17\n00:00:51,522 --> 00:00:52,899\nis enough to produce it.\n\n18\n00:00:53,069 --> 00:01:01,177\nWith better methods, about half of more than a thousand students and faculty judged their performance within ten percentage points.\n\n19\n00:01:01,665 --> 00:01:04,749\nAnd in a study of nine hundred and twenty nine adults,\n\n20\n00:01:05,012 --> 00:01:09,225\nself rated and measured intelligence lined up along a straight line,\n\n21\n00:01:09,642 --> 00:01:12,132\nwith no special blind spot at the bottom.\n\n22\n00:01:12,302 --> 00:01:15,734\nSo the safer lesson is not that some people are clueless.\n\n23\n00:01:16,073 --> 00:01:18,361\nEveryone's confidence is imperfect,\n\n24\n00:01:18,626 --> 00:01:21,677\nespecially in a skill you are just starting to learn.\n\n25\n00:01:22,219 --> 00:01:25,576\nDunning himself has said the effect is about all of us,\n\n26\n00:01:25,760 --> 00:01:26,939\nnot other people.\n\n27\n00:01:27,109 --> 00:01:30,063\nThe original paper also found something hopeful.\n\n28\n00:01:30,546 --> 00:01:32,475\nAfter brief training in logic,\n\n29\n00:01:32,814 --> 00:01:37,055\nlow scorers became far more accurate about their own performance.\n\n30\n00:01:37,225 --> 00:01:38,205\nSome caution.\n\n31\n00:01:38,627 --> 00:01:41,927\nResearchers still debate how much of the effect is real,\n\n32\n00:01:42,139 --> 00:01:46,454\nand some critics of the critiques argue the statistics cut both ways.\n\n33\n00:01:46,860 --> 00:01:51,773\nBut one fix works either way: compare your confidence with real results.\n\n34\n00:01:51,943 --> 00:01:52,768\nTry this.\n\n35\n00:01:53,043 --> 00:01:55,148\nBefore each quiz, predict your score,\n\n36\n00:01:55,168 --> 00:01:56,081\nthen compare.\n\n37\n00:01:56,421 --> 00:01:59,164\nMark the questions you felt sure about,\n\n38\n00:01:59,546 --> 00:02:00,256\nand check them.\n\n39\n00:02:00,276 --> 00:02:02,350\nGet feedback early, well before the exam.\n\n40\n00:02:02,617 --> 00:02:05,050\nAnd never use Dunning Kruger as an insult.\n\n41\n00:02:05,427 --> 00:02:06,879\nUse it as a mirror.\n\n42\n00:02:07,049 --> 00:02:09,664\nCheck your confidence against your results.\n\n"
